\subsection{Aggregation Rules Modulate the Diversity Channel}
\label{sec:aggregation}

Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} reports regression results for four aggregation methods across all 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{$\eta_{\max}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ $\beta$ dir.} & \textbf{$R^2$} \\
\midrule
Threshold       & 12/12 & 11/12 & 10+/1$-$  & 0.89 \\
Majority        & 11/12 & 8/12  & 7+/1$-$   & 0.298 \\
Weighted        & 9/12  & 9/12  & 9+/0$-$   & 0.284 \\
Bayesian        & 9/12  & 7/12  & 1+/6$-$   & 0.104 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Aggregation method comparison ($K{=}3$, 15 models, 455 panels, log-log canonical spec). Significance counts are FDR-corrected across 12 conditions. $R^2$ is the mean across conditions. Methods are ordered by $R^2$ to highlight the vulnerability gradient from threshold (most predictable) to Bayesian (least).}
\label{tab:aggregation}
\end{table}


The methods form a vulnerability gradient from threshold ($R^2{=}0.89$) to Bayesian ($R^2{=}0.104$; Figure in Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_details}). Bayesian calibration reverses the diversity channel: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ direction flips from predominantly positive (7+/1$-$) to predominantly negative (1+/6$-$). Threshold voting amplifies both channels ($\eta_{\max}$: 12/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$: 11/12) because unanimity makes panels maximally sensitive to composition.

